// **`@note` attributes** (ADR-0098): metadata a declaration carries for a *metaprogram* to read. W6's first
// sub-wave — the wave PLAN §2.1 calls "the Jai superpower", where build scripts become the build system.
//
// A note is `@name` or `@name "payload"` on a declaration: `@deprecated`, `@requires "a positive x"`. It is
// **not** an instruction to the compiler — that is what `#c_call` and `#no_abc` are — which is why it is its
// own node kind and its own list on the declaration. A consumer collecting notes must not have to filter
// directives out of the same list, and vice versa.
//
// **Why notes come first in W6.** They are the only self-contained piece of the wave, and they are the *data*
// the other three operate on: the compiler message loop exists to hand declarations to a build script, and a
// declaration with nothing extra to say is not worth handing over; a build script's first real job is
// "collect every declaration tagged `@X`". Building the loop first would mean designing its message shape
// against no consumer — the failure ADR-0080 §3 named for a `Code` value, "worth representing only once
// something can inspect it".
//
// What is checked here:
//
//   * a bare note (`@deprecated`);
//   * a note with a **string payload** (`@requires`), which is what makes the two spellings one form;
//   * **several** notes on one declaration, so the list is a list;
//   * a note **beside a directive**, in both orders — the attribute loop takes them in any order, the rule
//     ADR-0058 settled for the directives themselves;
//   * a note on a `#expand` **macro** and on a polymorphic `$T` procedure, so notes compose with W5.
//
// A note affects **no code**: this program's MIR is exactly what it would be without them, which is the point
// — a note is read by a metaprogram, not by the compiler. So `main` does ordinary work and exits 7.
#import "Basic";

/// A bare note.
old_way :: (x: s64) -> s64 @deprecated {
    return x;
}

/// A note with a payload — the same form, with the optional string present.
checked :: (x: s64) -> s64 @requires "a positive x" {
    return x;
}

/// Several notes on one declaration.
tracked :: (x: s64) -> s64 @deprecated @internal @since "0.2" {
    return x;
}

/// A note before a directive.
fast :: (x: s64) -> s64 @hot #no_abc {
    return x;
}

/// And after one, since the loop takes either order.
faster :: (x: s64) -> s64 #no_abc @hot {
    return x;
}

/// A note on a `#expand` macro — notes compose with W5's macros.
doubled :: (x: s64) -> s64 @inline #expand {
    return x * 2;
}

/// A note on a polymorphic procedure — and on one with a `$T`, so it composes with instantiation too.
identity :: (x: $T) -> T @generic {
    return x;
}

main :: () {
    n := identity(7);
    exit(n);
}
